ation alice and  bob do not talk to each other  instead there is a third party a referee  charlie to whom both alice and bob  send messages charlie upon receiving the  messages from alice and bob  needs to declare whether the function is  0 or 1.  sometimes we allow alice and bob to  share epr pairs  sometimes we don't it depends on the  model  these are successively weaker models of  communication  that is there are functions that are  easy in the two-way model  but hard in the one-way model similarly  there are functions that are easy in the  one-way model  but hard in the simultaneous model  now that i've introduced the relevant  communication models  let us state the main problem of  interest  is there a communication problem that  has a small cost quantum protocol  but no small cost classical protocol  is there a function that is easy to  communicate when you can exchange  qubits but hard when you can only  exchange classical bits  so does quantum provide advantage in the  world of communication complexity  for total functions we don't know the  answer it could very well be  that every quantum protocol for a total  function can be converted into a  classical protocol  with minimal overhead this is a major  open question  for partial functions on the other hand  there is a long line of research  spanning over 20 years  all of which demonstrates that quantum  communication can  provide more power that is quantum  communication can provide exponential  advantages over classical communication  for partial functions the first  such result was established by moorman  cleveland winterson  who showed that in the zero error  setting when we want to answer correctly  with probability one  there are partial functions that are  easy in the quantum simultaneous model  but exponentially harder in the  classical two-way model  this suggests that a rather weak model  of quantum communication  can exponentially outperform a rather  powerful classical model of  communication  in the bounded error setting when we  want to answer correctly with probably  at least  2 3 the first such result was  shown by ran raj he showed that quantum  2a can exponentially outperform  randomized  2a a similar result was shown for  quantum one-way versus randomized  one-way  this was strengthened to a separation  between quantum one-way and randomized  two-way  finally the state-of-the-art separation  is between quantum simultaneous where  alice and bob share entanglement  and randomized 2a let us take a closer  look at this result  the state of the art result is due to  govindski  he shows that there is a partial  function which has a small cost  quantum simultaneous protocol when alice  and bob share a few epr pairs  however there is no randomized protocol  of small cost regardless of the number  of rounds  this result essentially subsumes most of  the prior separations  on bounded error quantum versus bonded  error classical  secondly it demonstrates that a rather  weak model of quantum communication  can exponentially outperform a rather  powerful model of  classical communication  one drawback of this result in the  context of our work  is that the quantum protocol is not  efficient  we say that a quantum protocol is  efficient if it can be implemented by a  quantum circuit  of small size with oracle access to the  inputs  this means two things the first is that  the amount of post-processing and  pre-processing done by the players  is not too much in comparison to the  size of the messages that they send  that is everything that alice bob and  charlie do  can be implemented by quantum circuits  of poly logarithmic  size secondly the oracle access to the  inputs  just means that alice and bob don't need  to read their input bit by bit  they can make a few queries to their  input in superposition  and still be able to execute the  protocol  in govind's case result the quantum  protocol is not efficient  because the referee has to do in many  measurements one after the other  our result addresses this aspect  be sure that there is a partial function  which has an efficient  small cost quantum simultaneous protocol  when alice and bob share a few epr pairs  however there is no randomized protocol  of small cost  regardless of the number of rounds our  parameters are slightly weaker than  kavinsky's result  but the important difference is that the  quantum protocol in  our result is efficient this means  that everything that alice bob and  charlie do can be implemented by  circuits of  small size with oracle access to the  input  in other words we qualitatively recover  govinski's separation  between quantum simultaneous and  randomized two-way  furthermore our separation has the  property that the quantum protocol is  efficient  that is the actions of all players can  be implemented by circuits of  small size with oracle access to the  inputs  what did we know about efficient  separations  to the best of our knowledge this has  not been explicitly addressed  in the setting of quantum versus  classical separation  in most of the previous separations the  quantum parties seem to be inefficient  and they're inefficient in many  different ways one is that the referee  has to do a lot of  host processing this is the case in  govinsky's result  another reason is the players need to  make multiple probes into the input  this is the case for most of the other  separations that i mentioned  nevertheless we did have an efficient  separation prior to this work  this was between quantum two-way and  randomized two-way  this is obtained by taking the existent  efficient separations in the query  complexity world  and lifting them to communication  complexity using the query to  communication lifting theorems  the reason this only gives us an  efficient separation between two-way  quantum and two-way randomized  is that the gadgets used in these  lifting theorems  seem to introduce two new rounds into  the communication protocol  our result unites these two lines of  work we recover the state-of-the-art  result  using quantum protocols where all the  parties are efficient  secondly we provide a new lower bound  technique for randomized communication  complexity  based on fourier analytic techniques  from these two works  the first is the polarizing random walks  result of shadow padilla etta  the second is the oracle separation  result of bqp  and ph  now that i have introduced our results  let me demonstrate the function for  which our separation holds  our function is based on the hadamard  matrix  so let capital n b2 to the little end  the hadamard matrix of size capital n by  capital n  is defined as follows each row and  column  can be indexed by a bit string of length  little n  let a and b be bit strings of length  little n  the a comma b entry is plus or minus 1  over square root capital n  the sign is plus if the inner product  between the row and the column is 0 mod  2  and the sign is minus if the inner  product between the row and column is 1  mod 2.  here is an example of a 2 by 2 hadamard  matrix the hadamard matrix is a unitary  matrix  it has close connections with the  fourier transform so for instance  if you take the truth table of a  function and apply the hadamard matrix  what you get is the truth table of the  fourier transform of the function  up to some normalization factor we are  now ready to define the correlation of a  vector  the correlation of a vector is  essentially the inner product between  the second half of the vector  and the hadamard transform of the first  half up to some normalization  in other words it measures the  correlation between the second half of  the vector  and the fourier transform of the first  half  up to normalization  the correlation function was first  introduced by aaronson  who showed that estimating the  correlation of a vector  is easy when you have quantum query  access to the entries of the vector  but is difficult when you only have  classical query access  the correlation function has thus  already been used to separate  quantum and classical in the query  complexity world  we show a separation in the  communication complexity world  for convenience our inputs are now from  minus 1 1 instead of  0 1 and we use the pointwise product  instead of xor our problem is as follows  the correlation communication problem  alice gets x bob gets y their goal  is to find out if x x or y has high  correlation  or whether it has low correlation where  this high  and low differ by roughly 1 over log n  where n  is roughly the input size if you are  familiar with the lifting literature  this is a lift of the correlation query  problem with xor as the gadget  our main separation is for this function  that is we show that this function has  an efficient  quantum simultaneous protocol of small  cost when alice and bob share a few epr  pairs  however this function has no randomized  protocol of small cost  regardless of the number of rounds  this is our main theorem in the next 10  minutes or so  i will describe a proof of this main  theorem the quantum upper bound  essentially follows  from existing work we will instead focus  on  the proof of the randomized lower bound  by the yahoo's minmax principle it  suffices to produce two distributions  one on the yes instances of the  formulation problem and one on the no  instances  such that no deterministic protocols of  small cost can distinguish them with  considerable advantage  we now demonstrate our heart  distributions to do so we make use of  the forrelation distribution  as defined in the oracle separation  result of raj and tal  it's not too important what this  distribution is for the rest of the talk  but to have a picture in mind the  distribution is as follows  in the first step we sample a random  gaussian vector  where each entry has mean 0 and variance  epsilon  furthermore the second half is exactly  the hadamard transform of the first half  this vector is in some sense as for  related as can be  in the second step we perform a  randomized rounding of  z to get a boolean vector  the first thing that raj and tal show is  that a vector sampled from  this distribution has high correlation  on expectation  for now let us imagine that this holds  with high probability  we now define our heart distributions  for the communication problem  for the no instances a string z is  sampled  uniformly at random whereas for the yes  instances  z is sampled from the correlation  distribution  now in both cases a string x is sampled  uniformly at random  alice gets x bob gets x x or  z note that the distribution of no  instances  is the following distribution alice and  bob get two uniformly random strings  that are independent of  each other the distribution of yes  instances  is as follows alice and bob get two  uniformly random strings  conditioned on their xor being drawn  from the correlation distribution  note that when two strings are sampled  uniformly at random independently  their xor is also uniformly random  strings and  it has low correlation with high  probability intuitively speaking but  this can be formalized  on the other hand for the distribution  on yes instances the xor is distributed  according to the correlation  distribution  which i just mentioned has high  formulation  so these are valid hard distributions  and our main theorem is that  deterministic protocols of  small cost cannot distinguish these two  distributions with considerable  advantage  that is for all deterministic protocols  of cost at most c  their advantage in distinguishing these  two distributions is at most c  square over square root n in other words  when the cost is less than n to the one  fourth  the advantage in distinguishing these  two distributions is really small  in the next few minutes i will describe  an overview of the proof of this theorem  so our goal is to show that for  deterministic protocols of cost  at most c their advantage in  distinguishing these two distributions  is at most  c square over square root n every  protocol of cost c  can be broken up into two to see many  rectangles  this is a standard argument in  communication complexity  so it surfaces to show that rectangles  that are not too small  cannot distinguish these two  distributions that is  we want to show that for sets a and b  whose measure is roughly 2 to the minus  c  the advantage of the rectangle indicator  a times indicator b  in distinguishing these two  distributions is at most this quantity  which is c square over square root n  times 1  over 2 to the c  if we could prove this then by adding up  this inequality over all rectangles in  the partition we will get our desired  inequality  so now our goal is to show this  inequality  we introduce an important simplification  here which is the convolution of  functions the convolution of indicator  and indicator b  is denoted by indicator a star indicator  b  it's defined exactly as shown in this  equation  note that this quantity appears in both  of these terms  so we can substitute this back and  rewrite our goal as  follows so now our goal is to show  that the function indicator a star  indicator b has  similar expectations under the uniform  distribution and  under the formulation distribution  how do we go about showing this we make  use of existing  results  it follows from the works of chatopathy  and raj and tal  that if you want to show that a certain  function can't distinguish between the  uniform distribution and  the correlation distribution it suffices  to prove a  level 2 fourier bound on this function  to be more precise  we need a nice family of boolean  functions  this nice is some technical condition  which is closed under restrictions  this is satisfied by most of the  functions that you could think of that  are built out of  small cost protocols small cost decision  trees and so on  but let us just ignore this condition  for now this theorem says  if that for all functions in your family  the level two fourier mass  is at most alpha then the advantage that  this function family has  in distinguishing the uniform  distribution from the correlation  distribution  is at most alpha over square root n  that is if any nice family of boolean  functions has  small level 2 fourier mass it cannot  distinguish between the uniform  distribution  and a distribution built out of weakly  correlated gaussians  we make use of this result as follows so  our goal was to show  that indicator a star indicator b cannot  distinguish between the uniform  distribution  and a distribution built out of weakly  correlated gaussians  we reduce this to showing a bound on the  level 2 fourier mass of indicator a  star indicator b  so now our goal is to just prove this  fourier bound  how do we prove this fourier bound  the first observation is that the  fourier coefficients of the convolution  are the product of fourier coefficients  using this we can simplify the  expression for the level to mass  as follows we now apply cauchy schwartz  to obtain this  we now bound each of these terms in this  inequality  as follows we use the level k  inequalities  this appears as an application of hyper  contractivity in ryan odonnell's book  this allows us to bound the sum of  squares of level two fourier  coefficients  of an indicator function of a subset in  terms of the measure of the subset  this inequality and its variance have  been used in most of the previous  separations on quantum versus classical  communication complexity  so it is not surprising that it shows up  in our work as well  substituting this inequality in our  previous inequality we get exactly what  we want  this concludes the proof of the level 2  fourier bound  to summarize what we did we considered  this function f of z  which is obtained by taking a  convolution of low cost communication  protocols  we bounded the level two fourier mass of  that function and  we 